## Claim

Yes. For every $\mathbb{C}$-linear map $f: \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}) \to \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$, there exist matrices $A_1,\dots,A_d, B_1,\dots,B_d \in \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ such that
$$f(X) = \sum_{j=1}^d A_j X B_j \quad \forall\, X \in \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}).$$

---

## Proof

Let $\{E_{ab}\}_{1 \le a,b \le n}$ be the standard matrix units (the matrix with $1$ in position $(a,b)$ and $0$ elsewhere). This is a $\mathbb{C}$-basis of $\mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$, so $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}) = n^2$, and consequently
$$\dim_{\mathbb{C}} \operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}\!\big(\mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C})\big) = n^4.$$

**Define $n^4$ linear maps.** For each quadruple of indices $(i,j,k,l)$ with $1 \le i,j,k,l \le n$, define
$$T_{ij,kl} : \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}) \to \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}), \qquad T_{ij,kl}(X) := E_{ij}\, X\, E_{kl}.$$
Each $T_{ij,kl}$ is $\mathbb{C}$-linear and is of the "sandwich" form $X \mapsto A X B$ (with $A = E_{ij}$, $B = E_{kl}$).

**Compute the action explicitly.** For any $X = (x_{ab})$,
$$(E_{ij}\, X\, E_{kl})_{ab} = \sum_{p,q} (E_{ij})_{ap}\, x_{pq}\, (E_{kl})_{qb} = x_{jk}\, \delta_{ai}\, \delta_{bl}.$$
Hence
$$T_{ij,kl}(X) = E_{ij}\, X\, E_{kl} = x_{jk}\, E_{il}.$$
So $T_{ij,kl}$ extracts the single entry $x_{jk}$ from $X$ and places it in position $(i,l)$, producing a matrix that is zero everywhere else.

**Linear independence.** Suppose $\sum_{i,j,k,l} c_{ij,kl}\, T_{ij,kl} = 0$ as a linear map. Evaluating at $X = E_{jk}$ (so that $x_{jk} = 1$ and all other entries are $0$):
$$0 = \sum_{i,j,k,l} c_{ij,kl}\, T_{ij,kl}(E_{jk}) = \sum_{i,l} c_{ij,kl}\, E_{il}.$$
The matrices $\{E_{il}\}$ are linearly independent, so $c_{ij,kl} = 0$ for all $i,l$ (for this fixed $j,k$). Varying over all $j,k$, we get $c_{ij,kl} = 0$ for every quadruple. Thus the $n^4$ maps $\{T_{ij,kl}\}$ are linearly independent.

**They form a basis.** Since $\{T_{ij,kl}\}$ is a linearly independent set of size $n^4 = \dim \operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}))$, it is a basis of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}))$.

**Decompose $f$.** Since $\{T_{ij,kl}\}$ is a basis, any $\mathbb{C}$-linear $f$ can be written uniquely as
$$f = \sum_{i,j,k,l} c_{ij,kl}\, T_{ij,kl}$$
for some scalars $c_{ij,kl} \in \mathbb{C}$. Therefore, for every $X$,
$$f(X) = \sum_{i,j,k,l} c_{ij,kl}\, E_{ij}\, X\, E_{kl} = \sum_{i,j,k,l} (c_{ij,kl}\, E_{ij})\, X\, E_{kl}.$$

Setting $A_{(i,j,k,l)} := c_{ij,kl}\, E_{ij}$ and $B_{(i,j,k,l)} := E_{kl}$ (and re-indexing the $n^4$ quadruples by $j = 1, \dots, d$ with $d = n^4$), we obtain
$$f(X) = \sum_{j=1}^{d} A_j\, X\, B_j \quad \forall\, X \in \mathrm{M}_n(\mathbb{C}).$$

(One may reduce $d$ by combining terms, but existence with $d = n^4$ is already established.) $\blacksquare$

